
A special subroutine has been inserted into readgeometry.web defining 
H-alpha viewing chords and spectrum detectors. To allow these to be
included, set the GEOMETRY flag to WISING_CMOD prior to compilation. 
Note that this subroutine will work only for particular cases. It can
be adapted to other C-Mod geometries by appropriately shifting the
chord origin. Be sure to reset GEOMETRY to GENERAL before using readgeometry
for other runs.

The output provided here uses 99 flights for each of the three source groups
in bk_cmdetue.nc. The spectrum presented in Stotler's 1998 High Temperature
Plasma Diagnostics Conference paper (Reviews of Scientific Instruments,
1999) was generated using 10000 flights for the two plate sources and
50000 for the recombination source. Strictly speaking, the "collision"
estimator was used for recombination in the reaction tallies (mainly
the H-alpha spectrum) instead of "post-processing" (the Stark broadening
option hadn't been written yet). Also, a small error in the code at that
time meant that it was actually running one less flight than requested for
each source group.

This example is intended to document Stark and Doppler broadening of the
H-alpha spectrum. To turn on Stark broadening, set the compiler flag
STARK to 1 in score.web. Note that this should otherwise be 0 since it
slows down the code tremendously.

The geometry*.inp files are to be use as the geometry.inp files for
geomtesta.

Summary run procedure:

1. Copy to run directory (e.g., SUN or ALPHA):

   cp degas2.in cmoddet.u cmoddetD2 ../../SUN
   cd ../../SUN

2. Run datasetup (if needed; this run uses the standard reference set of
   atomic and surface physics data):

   gmake datasetup
   datasetup

3. Run problemsetup:

   gmake problemsetup
   problemsetup

4. Generate geometry input:

   (edit readgeometry.web as above)
   gmake readgeometry
   readgeometry cmoddetD2

5. Create background input file:

   gmake readbackground
   readbackground cmoddet.u
   (edit bk_cmdetue.nc to set desired number of flights)

6. Run tallysetup:

   gmake tallysetup
   tallysetup

7. Run main code:

   (set STARK flag in score.web)
   gmake flighttest
   flighttest
   (creates text output and main netCDF output file)

8. If desired, use geomtesta to create HDF files:

   (copy a geometry.inp file to working directory)
   gmake geomtesta
   geomtesta

NOTE: unlike most of the other examples, this case employs a realistic
geometry. Consequently, the roundoff error is noticeably larger. To get
the best possible comparison with these results, compile & run the
code (including the setup of all of the input files) in DEBUG mode.
You should be able to get differences from matchout to be ~ 1.e-10 or 
smaller.
